Two fixes, in the order that makes the second verifiable.

**1. smart-test: a `conftest.py` change selected no tests at all.**

`conftest.py` is neither a `test_`-prefixed test file nor package `src/`, so it matched neither `CHANGED_TEST_FILES` nor `CHANGED_SOURCE_FILES`. smart-test reported "no test-relevant files changed (docs/config only)", wrote a manifest with **0 selected tests**, and `ci.yml` skipped pytest.

This is INV-kinin's sibling one filename over, and strictly worse than the fixture case that item already fixed: an `autouse` fixture declared in a conftest governs EVERY test beneath it, not one gate's inputs.

A package conftest now selects that package's suite exactly as a fixture does. A ROOT conftest selects the root suite **and every package suite**, because pytest applies a rootdir conftest to everything collected beneath it. Four new tests, RED beforehand, read the pattern off the shipped script rather than restating it.

Measured on the very change that exposed it: **0 selected tests to 55** (53 tracker suites + 2 root smart-test suites), 3,033 passing.

**2. tracker tests: the host decided the test.**

`protected_config` treats the existence of `/etc/hypergumbo-tracker/` as the opt-in signal and refuses to fall back when the directory exists but holds no entry for the repo being loaded — deliberately, since silent fallback is the downgrade attack it was written to close. Tests building a repo under `tmp_path` inherited whatever the developer's machine had.

On an opted-in host, **13 tests fail** (`test_sync`'s whole `TestPreflightCheck`, `test_configure`, `test_setup`), all raising `ProtectedConfigError` from `load_config`. On a host that never opted in, all 13 pass. Same tree, same commit, two answers — and **CI never saw it**, because its containers have no `/etc/hypergumbo-tracker`. The host that hid the defect is the one that matters.

Fixed with one autouse fixture, `_isolate_protected_config_root`, sibling to the existing `_isolate_ops_journal` and carrying the same rationale. It pins `PROTECTED_ROOT` to a path guaranteed absent, so every test starts from "not opted in" on every host.

Deliberately **not** a skip-if-present guard — that would leave these tests untested on exactly the hosts running the configuration they are about. Deliberately a module-attribute patch rather than an env var, because there is none to use: the agent controls its own environment, so an env-redirectable root would reopen the hole.

583 pass, including all 13 and `test_protected_config`'s own opt-in tests, which confirms the override ordering still holds.

These were found during WI-tusav's full-suite run and are pre-existing — verified by reverting that work and re-running (11 still failed). Reported rather than routed around.

